 "icon": "🎋",
 "summaryEn": "<p>The title is in the writer's own tribal language. In Malayalam it means <b>“the taste that bamboo shoots and nevenchi gave”</b>. The writer is from an Adivasi (tribal) community in Kerala, and he tells us, in his own way of speaking, about two foods his people love.</p><p>First, <b>bamboo shoots</b>: you pick them, chop them, boil them with salt and turmeric, throw away the water (so the bitter taste goes), then fry them with mustard seeds, onion, curry leaves and hot chillies. Long ago, people fried them in animal fat.</p><p>Second, <b>nevenchi</b>: from the text it seems to be a small river creature with a shell (maybe a kind of river snail). In April, when the river gets low, the whole village goes to the forest stream — men, women and even tiny kids — and dives for nevenchi. Then they clean, boil and cook them with crab, papaya or pumpkin and lots of spice. Big idea: every community has its own healthy, tasty food and its own way of sharing work.</p>",
